  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is bone marrow?",
      "a": "Bone marrow is the soft tissue inside bones. Red marrow makes blood cells; yellow marrow is mostly fat and is found in the middle of adult long bones.",
      "asks": [
        "Where are blood cells made?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a blood stem cell?",
      "a": "A blood stem cell lives in the red bone marrow. It can divide to make more stem cells and cells that develop into every kind of blood cell: red cells, white cells and platelets.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the stem cell"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How long do red blood cells live?",
      "a": "About 120 days. They have no nucleus and slowly wear out, so the marrow keeps making new ones while the spleen and liver recycle the old ones.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the red blood cells"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How many blood cells does the body make?",
      "a": "An adult makes roughly 2 to 3 million red blood cells every second, plus large numbers of white cells and platelets, to replace the ones that wear out.",
      "asks": [
        "How fast are blood cells made?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Which bones have red marrow?",
      "a": "In adults it is mainly in flat bones such as the hip bones, breastbone, ribs, spine and skull, and in the ends of long bones. In babies almost all bones have red marrow.",
      "asks": [
        "Where is red bone marrow?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Red blood cells live for about 120 days.",
    "An adult makes about 2 to 3 million red blood cells every second.",
    "All blood cells develop from blood stem cells in the red bone marrow."
